.bd
hdformat
- probe and format a HDCA hard disk
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd
hdformat -p device
.br
.bd
hdformat -m model [ -c cylinders ] [ -h heads ] [ -s sectors ] device
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
Hdformat talks to the HDCA - the port-mapped Winchester controller of
the M10, M20 and M26 - through the driver's raw command ioctl, the same
door mwformat (1) uses for the HD-DMA.  The device named must be the
whole-disk slice, the
.bd
c
node, exactly as label (1) names it: hdac, hdbc, hdcc or hddc.
.sp
With -p, hdformat probes the drive's geometry rather than writing it.
A formatted drive carries its shape in its sector headers - four bytes
of head, track, sector and key on every sector - and the probe reads
them: it steps out one track at a time until a header reads back a
different track, selects each head until one stops matching, and reads a
whole revolution of headers to find the highest sector number.  The
three together are the drive's rectangular solid - cylinders by heads by
sectors - and its capacity.  A drive with no headers at all is not
formatted, and hdformat says so.
.sp
Without -p, hdformat formats the drive: it writes a sector header for
every sector of every head of every track, in the interleave the
controller's own formatter used.  The geometry is what a model name says
it is, overridden by any of -c, -h or -s that appear:
.sp
.in +10
.ti -10
-m model	The drive model.  m10 is 244 tracks, 4 heads, 21 sectors;
m20 is 244 tracks, 8 heads, 21 sectors; m26 is 202 tracks, 8 heads, 32
sectors.  These are the numbers FORMATHD carried, and a model is
nothing more - the driver reads none of them.
.sp
.ti -10
-c cylinders	The track count, overriding the model's.
.sp
.ti -10
-h heads	The head count, overriding the model's.
.sp
.ti -10
-s sectors	The sectors per track, overriding the model's.
.sp
.in -10
A geometry from no model and no flags is an error: name a model, or give
-c and -h.  The HDCA numbers tracks and sectors in one byte and selects
eight heads, so a track past 255, a head past 8 or a sector past 32
does not fit it.
.sp
The format writes headers and nothing else.  The data fields are left
alone, so a drive that already carries a label keeps it; a fresh drive
still needs label -w and mkfs (1) after the format before it can be
mounted.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
